# A 99 % accurate test says yes. Are you ill?
subtitle: "Why a positive on a 99 % accurate test can be a coin flip when the disease is rare, and why a second test settles it"
thumb: "band \"99 % accurate. Probably fine.\" note"
voices: {"a":"gemini:Sadachbia | a lively, friendly statistician unpicking a surprise; conversational","take":"lines"}

## A 99 % accurate test says yes. Are you ill?
    pieces grid #8f887c x 300 y 385 width 480 height 480 n 10 rows 10 of grid hidden true
    text caption "100 people like you" #8f887c x 300 y 655 font_size 28 hidden true
    mark mark_sick around grid_1 #b5482e kind circle hidden true
    mark mark_fp around grid_57 #8a5fa8 kind circle hidden true
    text lazy "always says “healthy”" x 770 y 520 font_size 30 hidden true
    mark lazy_x around lazy kind strike hidden true
    math odds tex "\\frac{\\text{ill}}{\\text{well}} = \\frac{1 \\times 0.99}{{99 \\times 0.01}} \\approx 1" size 40 colors {"0.99":"#b5482e","0.01":"#8a5fa8"} x 775 y 385 hidden true
    portrait portrait_thomas_bayes x 790 y 590 width 160 of "Thomas Bayes" cameo true hidden true
    shape sick_fill #b5482e shape rect x 84 y 601 width 40 height 40 style.fill #b5482e hidden true
    text plus1 "+" #ffffff x 84 y 601 font_size 34 hidden true
    shape fp_fill #8a5fa8 shape rect x 372 y 361 width 40 height 40 style.fill #8a5fa8 hidden true
    text plus2 "+" #ffffff x 372 y 361 font_size 34 hidden true
    shape c_sick #b5482e shape rect x 715 y 590 width 80 height 80 style.fill #b5482e hidden true
    text c_p1 "+" #ffffff x 715 y 590 font_size 50 hidden true
    shape c_fp #8a5fa8 shape rect x 835 y 590 width 80 height 80 style.fill #8a5fa8 hidden true
    text c_p2 "+" #ffffff x 835 y 590 font_size 50 hidden true
    text c_lab "retest these two" #8f887c x 775 y 510 font_size 28 hidden true

    card title "A 99 % accurate test says yes. Are you ill?"

Does a positive on a ninety-nine percent accurate test mean you are probably ill? First, what that one number hides. Here are a hundred people like you.
    draw grid caption

Say one person in a hundred has the disease. Just this one.
    draw mark_sick sick_fill

A test that tells everyone they are healthy is right about ninety-nine of these hundred. Ninety-nine percent accurate, and useless.
    draw lazy

So accuracy alone says little. A test has two numbers: how often it catches the sick, and how often it wrongly flags the healthy.
    draw lazy_x

    erase lazy lazy_x

A good test catches ninety-nine percent of the sick, so this person tests positive.
    draw plus1

It also flags one healthy person in a hundred. Among ninety-nine healthy people that is about one: this person, perfectly well.
    draw mark_fp fp_fill plus2

Two positive results, and only one of them is ill. Your positive is a coin flip.
    highlight grid_1 grid_57

    pause 0.4

Weigh the two ways to get a positive: one sick person, caught, against ninety-nine healthy people, times a one percent slip.
    draw odds

When the false-alarm rate is as large as the share who are ill, a positive is even odds, however good the test sounds.
    highlight odds part "{99 \\times 0.01}" color #8a5fa8

Now retest just these two, with a second test whose mistakes are independent. In this new crowd, one in two is ill.
    group crowd members ["c_sick","c_p1","c_fp","c_p2","c_lab"]

The healthy crowd has shrunk from ninety-nine to one. A second positive means ninety-nine to one: ninety-nine percent.
    morph odds tex "\\frac{\\text{ill}}{\\text{well}} = \\frac{1 \\times 0.99}{{1 \\times 0.01}} = 99"

The first positive didn't settle it. It picked out a smaller crowd, in which the same kind of test is finally convincing.
    highlight odds

    erase crowd

Thomas Bayes worked out this kind of updating; his essay was published in 1763, two years after his death, by his friend Richard Price.
    draw portrait_thomas_bayes

    erase portrait_thomas_bayes

    erase odds

    quiz "Disease: 1 in 50. False alarms: 1 in 50. A positive means…"
        right: About even odds: the share who are ill equals the false-alarm rate, so a positive is as likely a slip as a real case.
        * About 98% ill
        + About even odds
        * About 2% ill

